  two weeks ago was a confidential  interview I would describe it as a  foundational interview with deputy  director McCabe it really did ask  questions about who what when where with  respect to a lot of these decisions that  were made at the FBI and the Department  of Justice as well that we're out of the  ordinary or out of standard protocol and  so really he was able to provide us with  a number of answers that will be helpful  in identifying the people that we need  to talk to he also was not able to  provide substantive answers to some  other questions so we'll continue this  process ultimately I think chairman  Gowdy and I agree that we'd like to see  former director Comey come and maybe  attorney general Lynch come in to answer  some of these questions because there's  a factual basis to bring them in and  give them the opportunity to clear up  what appear to be some inconsistencies  between the testimony that they've  previously given to members of Congress  and the documents that have now surfaced  you know congressman Gowdy one of the  things that strikes me about what's  happened with the Department of Justice  and specifically the FBI is so many  abnormal abnormalities things that  things would never ever happen and in  any other situation but you've both been  prosecutors what is different about the  investigation that went into Hillary  Clinton and the decision the  announcement what is that laundry list  of things that is so different than what  normally happens during these  investigations  well Jason let's say at the outset the  difficult facts make for difficult  conclusions and and 2016 was a really  difficult year for the FBI and part of  that was because of decisions they made  and part of it was because of their tiny  conducting an investigation in the  throes of a presidential race but the  short answer a question is what was  different about the investigation in the  Secretary Clinton the answer is almost  everything I'm not interested in  litigating their decision not to charge  her just forget about that you can like  it you cannot like it no entity is too  big to provide oversight over and the  FBI and the Department of Justice are  strong enough and venerable enough to  withstand scrutiny so Johnny Johnny  mentioned one point if you have made the  decision not to charge someone in May of  2016 before you have interviewed two  dozen witnesses before you have  interviewed the target of the  investigation then you're gonna have to  explain how you reach that decision not  to charge before you ended the  investigation I want to know about the  immunity agreements I want to know why  you allow fact witnesses to sit in on  witness interviews I want to know why  you wanted those witness interviews to  be voluntary you know Johnny and every  other really good prosecutor knows that  you use the grand jury to interview  witnesses you don't let the witness set  them the ground rules  and say well I'm gonna have these  friends and family in the room and we're  gonna take a break at this table that's  not the way it's done for anyone else in  this country so I want to ask the FBI  did you do things differently in her  investigation and quite frankly Jason we  already know the answer the answer is  yes so then we're gonna ask why did you  do it differently in her case and again  I think the bureau and the Department of  Justice are big enough to withstand  those questions real quickly at - both  of you maybe starting with congressman  Radcliffe the integrity of the  Department of Justice and the FBI is  under attack what do we need to do to  turn that around what needs to happen  internally there to get the confidence  of the American people back well you  know I think part of the problem that  we've been looking at here and be people  that have created the issues as chairman  Gowdy has said it's been a bad 18 months  for the Department of Justice and the  FBI some of the people that made it a  really difficult time are gone now but  there are new folks and really what  we've got to do as members of Congress  is conduct the oversight necessary to  see whether or not they're being  effective in restoring the integrity of  the Department of Justice listen I think  chairman Gowdy and I agree we hope that  the FBI and DOJ officials like former  director Comey and attorney general  Lynch we we genuinely hope that they  have plausible explanations for all of  these unprecedented circumstances that  chairman Gowdy just described we hope  that they do because if they don't then  we really are facing a constitutional  crisis we have a situation where the  American people will really question the  integrity of the FBI and the Department  of Justice for decades to come and so  you know I really hope that these  investigations that we're conducting in  our role as congressional oversight can  shed some light on that  we're not prosecutors anymore I'm not  accusing anyone of crimes neither as  chairman Gowdy but we have an oversight  role on behalf of the American people as  their elected representatives and that's  what all of these investigations at the  Congressional level are really about no  that's this country is different because  we are self-critical we do look under  the hood and see what's going on we'd  speak truth to power
 

